See README.md in this directory for the current story: this directory now
hosts real, individually reviewed Hunspell dictionaries (each in its own
`<language_code>/` subdirectory, with its own `LICENSE` and `SOURCE`), pinned
to an exact upstream commit and redistributed here as independent, unmodified
data files — not compiled or linked into smaragd's own GPL-3.0-or-later
source or binary. `catalog.json` is the machine-readable index of the same
facts; `src/spellcheck.rs` reads it to drive Settings > Editor > Dictionaries.

`placeholders/en_US.aff`/`.dic` and `placeholders/nb_NO.aff`/`.dic` are a
different thing entirely: tiny (~20-word), hand-written word lists with no
licensing question of their own, `include_bytes!`-bundled directly into the
compiled binary so English/Norwegian have *something* working immediately,
before either real dictionary has been downloaded. Every other language does
nothing at all (flags no words) until its real dictionary is downloaded — see
`src/spellcheck.rs`'s module doc comment for the full explanation of why.

This file used to describe an earlier, since-superseded state (only two
placeholder dictionaries existed, no real ones were bundled anywhere, and
Norwegian was flagged license-blocked). That review was revisited: see
`nb_NO/SOURCE` and `catalog.json`'s `dictionary_source_policy` for why
redistributing it here, as a genuinely separate file, is fine even though its
own license (GPL-2.0-only) would be a real problem if it were ever combined
or linked into smaragd's own binary.
